From 66b49c4a197bb34d1271923e066d8ab2081078f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: primez <mpincoski@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 06:44:59 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] [TSReader] Resume the clock of each stream after a seek and
 at the start

An audio PES needs a PTS only every 0.7 s, and the frames of a PES without
a PTS follow the frames before. A parser reset cleared that clock, so after
a seek, or at the start, the audio frames up to the next PES with a PTS had
no PTS. A video PES without a PTS takes the timestamps of the PES before,
but after a seek the parser took the last PES header of the seek scan, so a
key frame in such a PES came with the PTS of a later frame.

The seek scan keeps the frames that the streams read. Each stream continues
with the first frame of a PES at its resume position, and the reader reads
on, up to the end of the segment, until the clock at that frame is known:
from the frame, or from the next PTS less the durations of the frames
between. After the reset, the stream takes the PES header timestamps and
the audio clock of a read without the reset (ElementaryStream::Clock).
The start of the reader finds the clocks the same way.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
